name: Directory Brute Force Detection via Rare URI and Z-Score
version: 1
date: '2025-09-16'
author: Saeed, Splunk
status: production
type: Anomaly
data_source:
  - Nginx
description: |
  This analytic detects potential **directory brute force attacks** against an Nginx web server.
  It works by identifying clients that request an unusually high number of rare URIs which 
  result in 4xx or 5xx HTTP status codes.  

  The rule calculates the number of unique URIs accessed per source IP, then applies a 
  **z-score statistical method** to determine whether the activity significantly deviates 
  from the baseline average.  

  To minimize false positives, the detection excludes traffic from known crawlers, whitelisted 
  IP ranges, and already blacklisted sources. It also enriches findings with **AbuseIPDB 
  reputation data**.  

  Sources flagged by this analytic are strong indicators of directory brute forcing activity, 
  which adversaries use to discover hidden files, admin panels, or sensitive endpoints. 
search: |
  |tstats summariesonly=true count(Web.uri_path) as tcount max(_time) as lastTime 
    from datamodel=Web where Web.vendor_product="Nginx Web Server" Web.status IN (4**,5**) 
    by Web.uri_path Web.src
  | `drop_dm_object_name(Web)`
  | rare uri_path by src lastTime limit=10000
  | eventstats dc(uri_path) as unique_uri by src
  | dedup src
  | eventstats avg(unique_uri) as mean stdev(unique_uri) as stdev
  | eval z_score = (unique_uri - mean) / stdev
  | where abs(z_score) > 20
  | lookup knowncrwalers subnet as src output subnet
  | where isnull(subnet)
  | lookup whitelist subnet as src output subnet
  | where isnull(subnet)
how_to_implement: |
  This detection requires the **Web datamodel** to be populated, typically via a 
  Technology Add-On (TA) for Nginx, Suricata, Palo Alto, or other web sources.  
  Ensure that the `uri_path`, `status`, and `src` fields are parsed correctly into the 
  datamodel. External lookup files (known crawlers, whitelist, blacklist) must also be 
  maintained and updated regularly.
known_false_positives: |
  Automated vulnerability scanners, security testing tools, or poorly configured 
  web applications may trigger this detection. To reduce noise, regularly update 
  whitelists and crawler lists.  
references:
